  "problem": "Given that\n$D=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\pi \\end{aligned}],$\nfind $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061D.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061D=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-6}} |  |",
  "worked_solution": "Given a function $f$ and a diagonal matrix $D$ with diagonal entries ${\\lambda }_{1},\u2026,{\\lambda }_{n},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(D)=[\\begin{aligned}f({\\lambda }_{1}) & \u22ef & 0 \\\\ \u22ee & \u22f1 & \u22ee \\\\ 0 & \u22ef & f({\\lambda }_{n})\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061D & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi ) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}\u2061(-\\pi ) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2\\pi )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
